Define the units and comparisons before choosing an allocation (@inquiry-design-units: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../metrology/calibration/Design-and-sequence.lingua#r_1DM0C485A4H25DSVDR611QZPXC") { r_0D6650DB3035D66F497D72ADE2
Begin with [State the question and the kind of evidence its answer needs](State-the-question-and-the-kind-of-evidence-its-answer-needs.lingua#r_0E928F81D1D0CA612417C8B8CA) and [Design comparisons that can distinguish competing explanations](Design-comparisons-that-can-distinguish-competing-explanations.lingua#r_0A7E63167B1C0C05400C73A84E). A question needs an outcome, conditions and a target. A design specifies how observations will be obtained so that the proposed comparison bears on that question. It is more than a list of measurements: it connects what can vary, what is controlled, what is assigned, what is observed and what claim is possible afterward.

For a paper investigation, suppose six labeled specimens can each be read using protocol P or protocol Q. The outcome is an indicated length in millimetres under declared conditions. Our target is the average change in indication caused by using Q instead of P for these six specimens. This is not yet a change in physical size, an accuracy comparison against a reference, or a claim about every specimen in a factory. Those would require different evidence. Specify the indication's resolution, reading rule and reference conditions in an actual study; the later toy calculation stipulates exact values solely to isolate allocation reasoning.

Distinguish an observation unit from an allocation unit. A specimen can produce ten readouts, while its protocol is assigned only once. Those ten readouts do not become ten independently assigned specimens. If an entire session receives one protocol, the session is the allocation unit, and all specimens within it share that assignment. Treating within-session readouts as independent assignments would exaggerate the design's independent comparisons. State the unit count separately from the readout count.

Random sampling chooses units from a population; random assignment chooses conditions for units already present. A complete list of six deliberately chosen specimens can support a comparison under randomized assignment while providing no probability sample of a larger population. Conversely, a random sample does not make a voluntarily chosen protocol independent of specimen properties. Generalizing beyond the sampled or assigned units requires an explicit argument, not the word random by itself. Reuse [A specimen, a sampling target, and a result about a population](../metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH) for the sampling target and exclusions.

A nuisance factor affects the observation without being the intended comparison. Time, operator, initial specimen state and instrument drift may be relevant. If every P reading is early and every Q reading late, a protocol contrast may also be a time contrast. Randomized order, repeated checks, physically appropriate reversal and blocking address different parts of this problem. A block groups comparable units before assignment; randomizing within each block preserves a local comparison. Blocking on a result chosen after looking at protocol outcomes would change the question and analysis. Randomization also does not erase carryover, interference between units or an ill-defined protocol.

**Read and design:** [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), sections 5.3.3, 5.3.3.1 and 5.3.3.2. Draw a six-row assignment sheet with unit identity, pre-assignment information, assigned protocol, planned order and recorded outcome. Compare complete balanced assignment with three predeclared pairs containing one P and one Q each. State which nuisance differences pairing is intended to address and which remain. The next shared calibration lesson illustrates levels, order, drift and independent checks; its full instrument-specific formation remains separate.

**Challenge:** change the allocation unit to a session. Explain why six specimen readings from one P session and one Q session do not supply three independent P/Q pairs. Propose an arrangement that could separate session and protocol influences. State what a missing reading or a changed protocol would prevent you from concluding; do not fill the gap with a convenient value.

**Continue:** [Design a calibration that can reveal its own weaknesses](../metrology/calibration/Design-and-sequence.lingua#r_1DM0C485A4H25DSVDR611QZPXC).

**Return:** [Scientific inquiry and practice](../Inquiry.lingua#r_75MNKZQKNB0XBG021SWWW2HASB).
